No, if you are using tRPC, you would generally **not** call `fetch(/api/abc)` for your tRPC procedures.

Let's clarify the roles of your files and how tRPC works with Next.js:

1.  **`api/abc/route.ts` (Next.js App Router API Route):**
    *   This defines a traditional REST-like API endpoint at the path `/api/abc`.
    *   If you create this file, you would make `fetch` requests directly to `/api/abc` (e.g., `fetch('/api/abc', { method: 'POST', body: JSON.stringify({ /* ... */ }) })`).
    *   This is *separate* from tRPC.

2.  **`server/` (tRPC Definitions):**
    *   This directory (e.g., `server/routers/`, `server/trpc.ts`, `server/index.ts`) is where you define your tRPC *routers*, *procedures* (queries, mutations), and your *context*.
    *   These files *do not* directly create HTTP endpoints. They define the *logic* and *types* of your API.

3.  **The tRPC Catch-All API Route (`api/trpc/[...trpc]/route.ts`):**
    *   This is the *single* Next.js API route that acts as the **entry point** for *all* your tRPC calls.
    *   It typically looks like this (simplified):

    ```typescript
    // app/api/trpc/[...trpc]/route.ts
    import { fetchRequestHandler } from '@trpc/server/adapters/fetch';
    import { appRouter } from '@/server/index'; // Your main tRPC router definition

    const handler = (req: Request) =>
      fetchRequestHandler({
        endpoint: '/api/trpc', // This is the base path for all tRPC calls
        req,
        router: appRouter,
        createContext: () => ({}), // Your tRPC context
      });

    export { handler as GET, handler as POST };
    ```

**How tRPC Client Calls Work:**

When you use the tRPC client on your frontend (e.g., within React components), it generates a proxy that makes internal HTTP requests to your *single tRPC catch-all endpoint*.

Instead of `fetch('/api/abc')`, you would use the tRPC client like this:

```typescript
// On your client-side component or utility file:
import { trpc } from '@/utils/trpc'; // Assuming you set up your tRPC client here

async function fetchData() {
  try {
    // If you have a 'posts' router with a 'getById' query in your tRPC setup
    const post = await trpc.posts.getById.query({ id: 123 });
    console.log(post);

    // If you have a 'users' router with a 'createUser' mutation
    const newUser = await trpc.users.createUser.mutate({ name: 'Alice', email: 'alice@example.com' });
    console.log(newUser);
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Failed to fetch data:', error);
  }
}

// Call it in a useEffect or on an event
// useEffect(() => { fetchData(); }, []);
```

**Summary:**

*   **You define your tRPC procedures in `server/`**.
*   **You have one Next.js API route (`api/trpc/[...trpc]/route.ts`) that handles *all* tRPC requests.**
*   **On the client, you interact with tRPC using the generated type-safe client (e.g., `trpc.yourRouter.yourProcedure.query()`), not direct `fetch` calls to individual endpoint paths.**

**Can you have both?**

Yes! You can perfectly have a mixed architecture:

*   Traditional Next.js API routes (`app/api/my-legacy-endpoint/route.ts`) for things that might be consumed by non-TypeScript clients, external services, or for specific file uploads/downloads that might not fit tRPC's paradigm perfectly.
*   tRPC (`app/api/trpc/[...trpc]/route.ts`) for all your internal, type-safe API interactions within your Next.js application.

So, if you called `fetch(/api/abc)` before, and you still have `app/api/abc/route.ts` defined, it will *still work* as a separate, traditional API route. But it will *not* be handled by your tRPC setup in `server/`.

Here's a complete HTML + JavaScript solution for your use case:

---

### 📁 Project Folder Structure:
Assume your local directory looks like this:

```
/catalog/
│
├── index.html
├── items.txt
├── images/
│   ├── item1.jpg
│   ├── item2.jpg
│   └── ... (more .jpg files)
```

- **images/**: contains the `.jpg` image files.
- **items.txt**: contains lines like `filename.jpg|Item description here`
- **index.html**: your main page.

---

### 🔧 Step-by-Step Implementation:

#### ✅ 1. Example `items.txt`:
```
item1.jpg|Red T-shirt, cotton, size M
item2.jpg|Wireless headphones with noise cancellation
item3.jpg|12" Non-stick frying pan
```

---

#### 💻 2. `index.html` (HTML + JavaScript):
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8" />
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0"/>
  <title>Item Catalog</title>
  <style>
    body { font-family: Arial, sans-serif; padding: 20px; }
    .item { border: 1px solid #ccc; padding: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px; display: flex; gap: 20px; align-items: center; }
    .item img { max-width: 150px; height: auto; }
    .item-description { flex-grow: 1; }
    input[type="email"] { width: 300px; padding: 5px; margin-bottom: 20px; }
    button { padding: 10px 20px; font-size: 16px; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>

  <h1>Item Selection</h1>

  <label for="email">Enter your email:</label><br>
  <input type="email" id="email" placeholder="example@example.com" required><br><br>

  <div id="items"></div>

  <button onclick="downloadSelected()">Send</button>

  <script>
    async function loadItems() {
      try {
        const response = await fetch('items.txt');
        const text = await response.text();
        const lines = text.trim().split('\n');
        const itemsContainer = document.getElementById('items');

        lines.forEach((line, index) => {
          const [filename, description] = line.split('|');
          const itemDiv = document.createElement('div');
          itemDiv.classList.add('item');

          itemDiv.innerHTML = `
            <img src="images/${filename}" alt="Item image">
            <div class="item-description">
              <p>${description}</p>
              <label>
                <input type="checkbox" class="item-checkbox" data-description="${description}">
                Interested
              </label>
            </div>
          `;

          itemsContainer.appendChild(itemDiv);
        });
      } catch (err) {
        console.error('Error loading items:', err);
      }
    }

    function downloadSelected() {
      const email = document.getElementById('email').value;
      const checkboxes = document.querySelectorAll('.item-checkbox:checked');

      if (!email) {
        alert('Please enter your email address.');
        return;
      }

      if (checkboxes.length === 0) {
        alert('Please select at least one item.');
        return;
      }

      // Create content for the file
      let message = `Thank you for your interest, ${email}!\n\nYou have selected the following items:\n\n`;

      checkboxes.forEach(cb => {
        message += `- ${cb.getAttribute('data-description')}\n`;
      });

      // Create downloadable text file
      const blob = new Blob([message], { type: 'text/plain' });
      const link = document.createElement('a');
      link.href = URL.createObjectURL(blob);
      link.download = 'selected_items.txt';
      document.body.appendChild(link);
      link.click();
      document.body.removeChild(link);
    }

    // Load items on page load
    window.onload = loadItems;
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

---

### 🚀 How It Works

1. Loads list from `items.txt` on page load.
2. Each item is parsed into an image + description + checkbox.
3. User enters their email and checks the items of interest.
4. Clicking "Send" generates a `.txt` file and downloads it.

> ❗ This solution does not **actually email** the contents (email sending would require a server-side solution like Node.js, PHP, or a backend API).

---

### 🔐 Optional Backend Feature:
If you'd like **actual email delivery**, let me know — I can walk you through server-side implementation with a tool like **Node.js + Nodemailer**, or a **no-code integration** using **Formspree** or **Zapier**.

Let me know if your images or `items.txt` are structured differently!

