Rendering large SVGs in React Leaflet, especially at high zoom levels (like beyond zoom level 18), can become very performance-intensive. SVGs are vector-based and can be expensive to render and manipulate in the DOM, especially when they contain a lot of elements or complex paths.

Here are several strategies to improve performance:

---

### ✅ 1. **Use Canvas or Raster Images Instead of SVG**
SVGs are great for scalability, but for performance, especially at high zoom levels, consider:

- **Pre-rendering floorplans as raster images (PNG, JPEG)** at different zoom levels.
- Use `ImageOverlay` in Leaflet to display them:
  ```jsx
  import { ImageOverlay } from 'react-leaflet';

  <ImageOverlay
    url="/floorplans/floor1.png"
    bounds={[[lat1, lng1], [lat2, lng2]]}
    opacity={1}
/>
  ```

- You can generate these images from your SVGs using tools like Inkscape, Illustrator, or even programmatically via headless Chrome or Puppeteer.

---

### ✅ 2. **Use `Pane` with Lower zIndex and Simplify SVG**
If you must use SVG:

- **Simplify the SVG**: Remove unnecessary groups, paths, and metadata.
- **Use a custom Leaflet pane** with a lower zIndex to avoid unnecessary re-renders:
  ```jsx
  import { useMap } from 'react-leaflet';

  const CustomPane = () => {
    const map = useMap();
    map.createPane('svgPane');
    map.getPane('svgPane').style.zIndex = 400; // lower than markers
    return null;
  };
  ```

- Then assign your SVG overlay to that pane.

---

### ✅ 3. **Throttle or Debounce Zoom Events**
If you're doing any updates or re-renders on zoom events, throttle them:

```jsx
import { useMapEvents } from 'react-leaflet';
import { useCallback } from 'react';
import debounce from 'lodash.debounce';

const ZoomHandler = ({ onZoom }) => {
  const debouncedZoom = useCallback(debounce(onZoom, 200), []);
  useMapEvents({
    zoomend: () => {
      debouncedZoom();
    },
  });
  return null;
};
```

---

### ✅ 4. **Use `useMemo` and `React.memo` to Avoid Re-renders**
If your SVG overlay is a React component, wrap it in `React.memo` and use `useMemo` to avoid unnecessary re-renders:

```jsx
const FloorplanOverlay = React.memo(({ svgData, bounds }) => {
  return (
    <SVGOverlay bounds={bounds}>
      <g dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: svgData }} />
    </SVGOverlay>
  );
});
```

---

### ✅ 5. **Use Tiled Images (Advanced)**
If your floorplans are very large, consider slicing them into tiles (like map tiles) and serving them via a tile server (e.g., using Leaflet’s `TileLayer`).

- Tools like [MapTiler](https://www.maptiler.com/) or [gdal2tiles](https://gdal.org/programs/gdal2tiles.html) can help.
- This is more complex but gives the best performance at high zoom levels.

---

### ✅ 6. **Hide or Replace SVG at High Zoom Levels**
If the SVG is not needed at high zoom levels, hide it or replace it with a simplified version:

```jsx
const map = useMap();
const zoom = map.getZoom();

if (zoom > 18) {
  return null; // or render a simplified version
}
```

---

### ✅ 7. **Use WebGL (e.g., Pixi.js or Deck.gl)**
For very complex vector rendering, consider using WebGL-based rendering libraries like:

- [Pixi.js with Leaflet](https://github.com/manubb/Leaflet.PixiOverlay)
- [Deck.gl](https://deck.gl/) with Mapbox or Leaflet

This is a more advanced solution but can handle thousands of vector elements smoothly.

---

### Summary

| Strategy | Performance Gain | Complexity |
|---------|------------------|------------|
| Convert SVG to raster image | ✅✅✅ | Low |
| Simplify SVG & use custom pane | ✅✅ | Low |
| Throttle zoom events | ✅ | Low |
| Memoize components | ✅ | Low |
| Use tiled images | ✅✅✅ | High |
| Use WebGL (Pixi.js, Deck.gl) | ✅✅✅ | High |

---

Let me know if you want help converting your SVGs to raster or tiling them!

Sure! Below is a minimal but functional blog CMS written in Rust using the Axum web framework and PostgreSQL as the database. This CMS includes:

- A front page that lists blog posts with title and summary.
- Pagination support.
- A post page that shows the full content of a blog post.

🦀 Tech Stack:

- Rust
- Axum (web framework)
- SQLx (async PostgreSQL driver)
- PostgreSQL
- Tera (template engine)

📁 Project Structure:

.
├── Cargo.toml
├── .env
├── src
│   ├── main.rs
│   ├── db.rs
│   ├── handlers.rs
│   ├── models.rs
│   └── templates/
│       ├── index.html
│       └── post.html

Step-by-step Implementation

1. Cargo.toml

[package]
name = "blog_cms"
version = "0.1.0"
edition = "2021"

[dependencies]
axum = "0.7"
tokio = { version = "1", features = ["full"] }
sqlx = { version = "0.7", features = ["runtime-tokio", "postgres", "macros", "uuid", "chrono"] }
serde = { version = "1", features = ["derive"] }
serde_json = "1"
tera = "1.19"
dotenvy = "0.15"
uuid = { version = "1", features = ["v4"] }
chrono = { version = "0.4", features = ["serde"] }
tower = "0.4"
tower-http = { version = "0.4", features = ["trace"] }
tracing = "0.1"
tracing-subscriber = "0.3"

2. .env

DATABASE_URL=postgres://username:password@localhost/blog_cms

3. Database Schema

Run this SQL to create the posts table:

CREATE TABLE posts (
    id UUID PRIMARY KEY,
    title TEXT NOT NULL,
    summary TEXT NOT NULL,
    content TEXT NOT NULL,
    created_at TIMESTAMP NOT NULL DEFAULT NOW()
);

4. src/db.rs

use sqlx::{PgPool, postgres::PgPoolOptions};
use std::env;

pub async fn init_db() -> PgPool {
    let database_url = env::var("DATABASE_URL").expect("DATABASE_URL must be set");
    PgPoolOptions::new()
        .max_connections(5)
        .connect(&database_url)
        .await
        .expect("Failed to connect to database")
}

5. src/models.rs

use serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};
use sqlx::FromRow;
use uuid::Uuid;
use chrono::{DateTime, Utc};

#[derive(Debug, Serialize, FromRow)]
pub struct Post {
    pub id: Uuid,
    pub title: String,
    pub summary: String,
    pub content: String,
    pub created_at: DateTime<Utc>,
}

6. src/handlers.rs

use axum::{
    extract::{Path, Query, State},
    response::Html,
};
use std::collections::HashMap;
use tera::Tera;
use uuid::Uuid;

use crate::{models::Post, AppState};

#[derive(Debug, Deserialize)]
pub struct Pagination {
    pub page: Option<usize>,
}

pub async fn list_posts(
    State(state): State<AppState>,
    Query(pagination): Query<Pagination>,
) -> Html<String> {
    let page = pagination.page.unwrap_or(1);
    let per_page = 5;
    let offset = (page - 1) * per_page;

    let posts = sqlx::query_as::<_, Post>(
        "SELECT * FROM posts ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT $1 OFFSET $2",
    )
    .bind(per_page as i64)
    .bind(offset as i64)
    .fetch_all(&state.db)
    .await
    .unwrap();

    let mut context = tera::Context::new();
    context.insert("posts", &posts);
    context.insert("page", &page);

    let rendered = state
        .templates
        .render("index.html", &context)
        .unwrap();

    Html(rendered)
}

pub async fn show_post(
    State(state): State<AppState>,
    Path(id): Path<Uuid>,
) -> Html<String> {
    let post = sqlx::query_as::<_, Post>("SELECT * FROM posts WHERE id = $1")
        .bind(id)
        .fetch_one(&state.db)
        .await
        .unwrap();

    let mut context = tera::Context::new();
    context.insert("post", &post);

    let rendered = state
        .templates
        .render("post.html", &context)
        .unwrap();

    Html(rendered)
}

7. src/main.rs

mod db;
mod handlers;
mod models;

use axum::{
    routing::get,
    Router,
};
use std::net::SocketAddr;
use tera::Tera;
use dotenvy::dotenv;
use std::sync::Arc;
use sqlx::PgPool;

#[derive(Clone)]
pub struct AppState {
    pub db: PgPool,
    pub templates: Arc<Tera>,
}

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() {
    dotenv().ok();
    tracing_subscriber::fmt::init();

    let db = db::init_db().await;
    let templates = Tera::new("src/templates/**/*").unwrap();

    let state = AppState {
        db,
        templates: Arc::new(templates),
    };

    let app = Router::new()
        .route("/", get(handlers::list_posts))
        .route("/post/:id", get(handlers::show_post))
        .with_state(state);

    let addr = SocketAddr::from(([127, 0, 0, 1], 3000));
    println!("Listening on http://{}", addr);
    axum::Server::bind(&addr)
        .serve(app.into_make_service())
        .await
        .unwrap();
}

8. src/templates/index.html

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>My Blog</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Blog Posts</h1>
    {% for post in posts %}
        <div>
            <h2><a href="/post/{{ post.id }}">{{ post.title }}</a></h2>
            <p>{{ post.summary }}</p>
        </div>
    {% endfor %}

    <div>
        <a href="/?page={{ page | minus(1) }}">Previous</a> |
        <a href="/?page={{ page | plus(1) }}">Next</a>
    </div>
</body>
</html>

9. src/templates/post.html

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>{{ post.title }}</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>{{ post.title }}</h1>
    <p>{{ post.content }}</p>
    <a href="/">Back to list</a>
</body>
</html>

