Sure! If you're working with Java and CSV files for testing purposes, the `CSVParser` class (typically from libraries like **Apache Commons CSV**) can be very useful to read and parse CSV files in unit tests or integration tests.

Here are a few **examples using Apache Commons CSV** to demonstrate how to use `CSVParser` for Java testing. If you're not yet using it, you can add the Maven dependency like this:

---

### ✅ Maven Dependency for Apache Commons CSV

```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.apache.commons</groupId>
    <artifactId>commons-csv</artifactId>
    <version>1.10.0</version> <!-- Use the latest version available -->
</dependency>
```

---

## 📘 Example 1: Parse a Sample CSV String
This is useful for **unit tests** without needing actual files.

```java
import org.apache.commons.csv.CSVFormat;
import org.apache.commons.csv.CSVParser;
import org.apache.commons.csv.CSVRecord;

import java.io.StringReader;
import java.util.List;

public class CSVParserExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        String csvData = "Name, Age, Country\n"
                       + "Alice, 30, USA\n"
                       + "Bob, 25, UK";

        CSVParser parser = CSVParser.parse(
            new StringReader(csvData),
            CSVFormat.DEFAULT.withFirstRecordAsHeader().withTrim()
        );

        for (CSVRecord record : parser) {
            System.out.println("Name: " + record.get("Name"));
            System.out.println("Age: " + record.get("Age"));
            System.out.println("Country: " + record.get("Country"));
        }
    }
}
```

---

## 📘 Example 2: Load CSV for Testing from Classpath

You might have a file like `test-data.csv` under `src/test/resources`.

📄 Example `test-data.csv`:

```csv
ID, Product, Price
1, Laptop, 1200
2, Mouse, 25
```

🔍 Load CSV in a test:

```java
import org.apache.commons.csv.CSVFormat;
import org.apache.commons.csv.CSVParser;
import org.apache.commons.csv.CSVRecord;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;

import java.io.InputStreamReader;
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;
import java.util.List;

import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertEquals;

public class ProductRepositoryTest {

    @Test
    public void testLoadProductsFromCSV() throws Exception {
        try (InputStreamReader reader = new InputStreamReader(
                getClass().getResourceAsStream("/test-data.csv"), StandardCharsets.UTF_8)) {

            CSVParser parser = CSVFormat.DEFAULT
                    .withFirstRecordAsHeader()
                    .withTrim()
                    .parse(reader);

            List<CSVRecord> records = parser.getRecords();

            assertEquals(2, records.size());
            assertEquals("Laptop", records.get(0).get("Product"));
            assertEquals("25", records.get(1).get("Price"));
        }
    }
}
```

---

## 📘 Example 3: Parameterized JUnit 5 Tests with CSV

JUnit 5 supports parameterized tests with CSV source:

```java
import org.junit.jupiter.params.ParameterizedTest;
import org.junit.jupiter.params.provider.CsvSource;

import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertEquals;

public class MathTest {

    @ParameterizedTest
    @CsvSource({
        "1, 1, 2",
        "2, 3, 5",
        "10, 5, 15"
    })
    void testAddition(int a, int b, int expectedSum) {
        assertEquals(expectedSum, a + b);
    }
}
```

---

## ✅ Summary

| What | How |
|------|-----|
| **In-memory string CSV** | Use `StringReader` with `CSVParser` |
| **File-based CSV (e.g., for integration tests)** | Load from `resources` using `InputStreamReader` |
| **JUnit parameterized CSV tests** | Use `@CsvSource` or `@CsvFileSource` |
| **Header handling** | Use `.withFirstRecordAsHeader()` |
| **Whitespace trimming** | Use `.withTrim()` |

---

Let me know if you'd like examples using other libraries like **OpenCSV** or **Jackson** for CSV parsing instead.

Sure! Below is a **minimal 3D racing game** in **one HTML file**, using **`three.js` via CDN**, with a **synthwave aesthetic**. It requires **no npm or build tools**, just drop it into a `.html` file and open in your browser.

---

### 🚗 Features:
- 3D car model (basic box)
- Infinite road illusion
- Neon-style synthwave colors
- WASD/arrow key controls

---

### ✅ How to Use:
1. Create a file called `synthwave-racer.html`.
2. Paste the code below.
3. Open in your browser.

---

### 🎮 One-File Synthwave 3D Racing Game
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>Synthwave Racer</title>
  <style>
    html, body { margin: 0; overflow: hidden; background: black; }
    canvas { display: block; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.160.0/build/three.min.js"></script>
  <script>
    // ==== SETUP SCENE ====
    const scene = new THREE.Scene();
    scene.fog = new THREE.Fog(0x0a0a1f, 10, 100);

    const camera = new THREE.PerspectiveCamera(75, window.innerWidth/window.innerHeight, 0.1, 1000);
    camera.position.set(0, 5, 10);
    camera.lookAt(0, 0, 0);

    const renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({ antialias: true });
    renderer.setSize(window.innerWidth, window.innerHeight);
    document.body.appendChild(renderer.domElement);

    // ==== LIGHTING ====
    const ambientLight = new THREE.AmbientLight(0xff00ff, 0.5);  // synthwave pink
    scene.add(ambientLight);

    const dirLight = new THREE.DirectionalLight(0x00ffff, 1);    // synthwave cyan
    dirLight.position.set(0, 20, 10);
    scene.add(dirLight);

    // ==== GROUND ====
    const roadMaterial = new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0x111122, metalness: 0.3, roughness: 0.7 });
    const road = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.PlaneGeometry(20, 1000), roadMaterial);
    road.rotation.x = -Math.PI / 2;
    road.position.z = -500;
    scene.add(road);

    // ==== CAR ====
    const car = new THREE.Group();
    const body = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.BoxGeometry(1, 0.5, 2), new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0xff00ff }));
    body.position.y = 0.25;
    car.add(body);
    car.position.y = 0.25;
    scene.add(car);

    // ==== LINES ON ROAD ====
    const lineMaterial = new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({ color: 0xffff00 }); // neon yellow
    for (let i = 0; i < 100; i++) {
      const line = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.BoxGeometry(0.2, 0.01, 4), lineMaterial);
      line.position.set(0, 0.01, -i * 10);
      scene.add(line);
    }

    // ==== MOVEMENT ====
    const keys = {};
    window.addEventListener('keydown', e => keys[e.key.toLowerCase()] = true);
    window.addEventListener('keyup', e => keys[e.key.toLowerCase()] = false);

    let roadSpeed = 0.5;

    // ==== ANIMATION LOOP ====
    function animate() {
      requestAnimationFrame(animate);

      // Controls
      if (keys['a'] || keys['arrowleft'])  car.position.x -= 0.2;
      if (keys['d'] || keys['arrowright']) car.position.x += 0.2;
      car.position.x = THREE.MathUtils.clamp(car.position.x, -8, 8);

      // Infinite road effect by moving road lines
      scene.children.forEach(obj => {
        if (obj.geometry && obj.geometry.type === 'BoxGeometry' && obj !== car) {
          obj.position.z += roadSpeed;
          if (obj.position.z > 10) obj.position.z = -1000;
        }
      });

      // Camera follow
      camera.position.x = car.position.x * 0.5;
      camera.lookAt(car.position.x, 0, car.position.z);

      renderer.render(scene, camera);
    }

    animate();

    // ==== HANDLE RESIZE ====
    window.addEventListener('resize', () => {
      camera.aspect = window.innerWidth/window.innerHeight;
      camera.updateProjectionMatrix();
      renderer.setSize(window.innerWidth, window.innerHeight);
    });
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

---

### 🎨 Synthwave Aesthetic Tips:
- Use **neon pinks, purples, cyans, yellows**
- Dark background (`#000011`, `#0a0a1f`)
- Simple geometry with glow-like colors
- Use fog and directional lighting for depth

---

### 🔧 Want to Enhance It?
- Add collision and obstacles
- Use particle effects for tail lights
- Add synthwave BGM via `<audio>`
- Starscape skybox
- Trail shaders

